window.docContents[6].resolve({"/Basic-Types/Fixed-Precision-Integers/#fixed-ints":{"contents":"Lean's standard library includes the usual assortment of fixed-width integer types.\nFrom the perspective of formalization and proofs, these types are wrappers around bitvectors of the appropriate size; the wrappers ensure that the correct implementations of e.g. arithmetic operations are applied.\nIn compiled code, they are represented efficiently: the compiler has special support for them, as it does for other fundamental types.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Basic Types","header":"21.4. Fixed-Precision Integers","id":"/Basic-Types/Fixed-Precision-Integers/#fixed-ints"},"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#lake-comparator-sandbox":{"contents":"Unless --inadvisably-no-sandbox is specified, both comparator and check use bubblewrap to restrict the untrusted code's access to files and the network:* The root filesystem is read-only, so the code may use tools and libraries as usual but not modify the system.* Home directories are hidden, so none of the invoking user's files, credentials, or caches are readable.* Writes are confined to the project's .lake directory.* Only dependency resolution has network access, which it needs to fetch git dependencies.\nThe build, the export, and any external checkers run without a network.\n\nBecause home directories are hidden, dependencies that can be fetched only with the user's own credentials are unavailable inside the sandbox.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Build Tools\u0009Lake\u0009Command-Line Interface\u0009Challenges and External Checkers","header":"25.1.5.8.2. Sandbox","id":"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#lake-comparator-sandbox"},"/Iterators/Iterator-Definitions/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Iterators--Iterator-Definitions--Loops":{"contents":"IteratorLoop α m provides efficient implementations of loop-based consumers for α-based\niterators. The basis is a ForIn-style loop construct.Its behavior for well-founded loops is fully characterized by the LawfulIteratorLoop type class.This class is experimental and users of the iterator API should not explicitly depend on it.\nThey can, however, assume that consumers that require an instance will work for all iterators\nprovided by the standard library.Iteration over the iterator it in the manner expected by for loops.\n\nThis is the default implementation of the IteratorLoop class.\nIt simply iterates through the iterator using IterM.step. For certain iterators, more efficient\nimplementations are possible and should be used instead.\n\nAsserts that a given IteratorLoop instance is equal to IteratorLoop.defaultImplementation.\n(Even though equal, the given instance might be vastly more efficient.)The implementation of IteratorLoop.forIn in i is equal to the default implementation.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Iterators\u0009Iterator Definitions","header":"23.2.4. Loops","id":"/Iterators/Iterator-Definitions/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Iterators--Iterator-Definitions--Loops"},"/The--grind--tactic/Linear-Integer-Arithmetic/#cutsat-qlia":{"contents":"The solver is complete for linear integer arithmetic.\nHowever, the search can become vast with very few constraints, but the solver was not designed to perform massive case-analysis.\nThe qlia option to grind reduces the search space by instructing the solver to accept rational solutions.\nWith this option, the solver is likely to be faster, but it is incomplete.\n\nRational SolutionsThe following example has a rational solution, but does not have integer solutions:example {x y : Int} :\n    27 ≤ 13 * x + 11 * y →\n    13 * x + 11 * y ≤ 30 →\n    -10 ≤ 9 * x - 7 * y →\n    9 * x - 7 * y > 4 := by\n  grind\nBecause it uses the rational solution, grind fails to refute the negation of the goal when +qlia is specified:example {x y : Int} :\n    27 ≤ 13 * x + 11 * y →\n    13 * x + 11 * y ≤ 30 →\n    -10 ≤ 9 * x - 7 * y →\n    9 * x - 7 * y > 4 := by\n  grind +qlia\n`grind` failed\ncase grind\nx y : Int\nh : -13 * x + -11 * y + 27 ≤ 0\nh_1 : 13 * x + 11 * y + -30 ≤ 0\nh_2 : -9 * x + 7 * y + -10 ≤ 0\nh_3 : 9 * x + -7 * y + -4 ≤ 0\n⊢ False\n[grind] Goal diagnostics\n  [facts] Asserted facts\n  [eqc] True propositions\n  [cutsat] Assignment satisfying linear constraints\n    [assign] x := 62/117\n    [assign] y := 2\nThe rational model constructed by the solver is in the section Assignment satisfying linear constraints in the goal diagnostics.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009The  grind  tactic\u0009Linear Integer Arithmetic","header":"16.9.1. Rational Solutions","id":"/The--grind--tactic/Linear-Integer-Arithmetic/#cutsat-qlia"},"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-_LPAR_2026-11-16_RPAR_--Library":{"contents":"* #15654\nfixes a memory leak in String.toByteArray and String.toUTF8 that has been present for a long time but was uncovered by a recently added test.* #15581\nredefines the order on String to be the lexicographic order of the UTF-8 encoding, not of the character list.* #15659\nexports the lemmas declared in Init.Data.Int.DivMod.Pow.* #15651\nadds String.fromUTF8Lossy, a function that attempts to parse a ByteArray into a String, replacing invalid UTF-8 with the Unicode replacement character in a certain well-defined way.* #15647\nadds missing simprocs for bitgwise operations on UIntX/IntX.* #15628\nmakes Lean.Json.parse reject JSON objects that contain the same key twice (compared after unescaping), instead of silently keeping the last value.* #15594\nrenames error function names and other helper functions.* #15547\nadd RAII-based scoped lock guard* #15545\nremoves the allocation of the libuv handle in uv sockets, timer and signal objects.* #15571\nfixes how 0 is handled in the LT JsonNumber instance. Before the fix, (0 : JsonNumber) < 5e-2 would evaluate to false.* #15523\nfixes crashes in libuv timers and signals:* One shot timer and signal callbacks resolved their promise before releasing the event loop's reference to the handle (sync := true) caused this.* libuv treats a repeat period of 0 as a oneshot timer, so a repeating Timer with a 0 ms period fired once and every later promise stayed pending. It now ticks every millisecond.* Signal.cancel only drops the pending promise and keeps the handler listening, and a signal that arrives with no promise pending resolves the promise of the next next.* Signal waiters resolve with the number from Signal.toInt32 on every platform, instead of the operating system's number for that signal.* #15469\nmakes the Std.Http server generate its Date header in the HTTP-date format of RFC 9110 §5.6.7, for example Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT, instead of with a numeric offset (+0000). Clients that parse HTTP dates strictly, such as Apache HttpClient's cache, Go's http.ParseTime and Rust's httpdate, did not accept the previous form.* #15533\nreimplements String.toList in Lean. In doing so it also removes all hints that\nString.data ever existed as it has now almost been a year since its deprecation.* #15443\nmakes the Std.Http server send responses on systems without a timezone database (for example the ubuntu:24.04 Docker image, which does not ship tzdata). Previously the server closed every connection without a response, because generating the Date header looked up UTC in the zoneinfo files.* #15517\nchanges the index of Lean.Order.iSup and Lean.Order.iInf to {ι : Sort v}, so ⨆ h : P, f h over a proposition P elaborates. The lemmas le_iSup, iSup_le, iInf_le, le_iInf, iSup_apply and iInf_apply take the same Sort index.* #15002\nadds HTML-like syntax to Lean.Data.Html.Syntax, together with an elaborator from the syntax into Lean.Data.Html.* #15175\nstops Loop.configure from leaking and rejects System.random values above 2^31-1 before allocating a huge array.* #14935\nadds a type Lean.Data.Html of HTML forests — aimed primarily at authoring HTML (as opposed to internal representation in a web server, etc) — as well as a renderer that produces a String object.* #15329\nadds efficient LE and Ord instances for ByteArray and deduces all other order instances from that.* #15316\nremoves the duplicate BEq (Option a) instance derived in Init.Data.Option.Basic and instead makes the one derived in Init.Core exposed.* #15315\nchanges the attributes at Array.compareLex and Vector.compareLex to make sure that they actually get specialized. Previously, the comparison function was just passed by closure.* #15304\nadds the missing order instances for List, Array and Vector.* #15299\nmakes grind, and with it vcgen … with finish, prove goals about assertions that use lattice operations more reliably. For example, the nodup proof of the vcgen tutorial now closes with vcgen … with finish, as it used to with mvgen.* #15414\ntunes down some overeager grind e-matching annotations that would over-eagerly trigger cross-theory reasoning.* #15387\nadds lemmas to the lattice theory in Std.Internal.Order and computation rules for predicate transformers.* #15192\nfixes ContextAsync.race and ContextAsync.raceAll,ContextAsync.run and ContextAsync.background cancellations and Notify.notifyOne no longer spends its wakeup on a waiter whose select was already decided.* #15274\nadds the missing order instances on BitVec.* #15174\nsolves multiple issues in the TCP and UDP modules.* #15266\nadds logical models for the Float.minimum and Float.minimumNumber operations that were added in #15257.* #15257\ndefines Float.minimum and Float.minimumNumber, which correspond to the IEEE-754-2019 operations of the same name. It redefines Min Float to mean Float.minimum.* #15115\nexposes Char.ordinal, Char.ofOrdinal, Char.succ? and Char.succMany?, so that they reduce across module boundaries. Ranges over Char are built from Char.succ? and Char.succMany? via the UpwardEnumerable Char instance, so previously cbv and kernel reduction got stuck on these in any file using the module system.* #15247\nintroduces a basic FFI module for CaDiCaL as well as a small wrapper around it to interact with our Std.Sat API.* #15252\nremoves the withPtrEq function.* #15217\nrenames the EPred type variable to EPosts and the epost value variable to eposts, since they denote a bundle of exception postconditions rather than a single predicate, and adds deprecated_arg aliases so code passing EPred by name keeps compiling.* #15171\nrenames several Std.WP names for consistency and deprecates the old ones. The WP.wpTrans projection becomes WP.trans, its monotonicity field WP.wp_trans_monotone becomes WP.trans_monotone, and the wpTrans_apply_eq lemma becomes trans_apply_eq. The weakest-precondition monotonicity lemmas WP.wp_consequence/wp_econs become a uniform wp_monotone family: wp_monotone for both channels, wp_monotone_post and wp_monotone_epost for one channel, wp_monotone_bot for the empty exception postcondition, and the _le transitivity variants.* #15201\nrefactors the AIG to CNF transformation from only acting on AIG Nat to acting on generic AIG types. This has two advantages:1. We don't have to perform the relabelNat step anymore and the CNF representation gets slightly more efficient. This is because instead of using the Nat from the AIG atom as the identifier we now use the indices into the AIG as an identifier and thus avoid producing one tseitin atom per AIG atom.2. In the future we will be able to use the AIG to CNF API in an incremental fashion across multiple runs. Towards this we also already introduce the State.cast API for reusing a cache state across runs.* #15067\nextends the frame mechanism to the exception channel of Std.WP. For example, a program logic whose exceptional exits carry state now supports a frame rule such as ⦃P⦄ x ⦃Q; E⦄ → ⦃F ⋆ P⦄ x ⦃F ⋆ Q; F ⋆ E⦄. The frame closure, the frame condition, and the frame rule gain a companion operator opE : R → EPred → EPred for the exception postcondition that defaults to ignoring the frame.* #14793\nfixes #14776* #14795\nfixes two issues in recv functions of the UDP and TCP modules.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.36.0 (2026-11-16)","header":"Library","id":"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-_LPAR_2026-11-16_RPAR_--Library"}});